<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Which USB accessories this app claims.

  The bare <usb-accessory /> matches everything, and it is deliberate.

  AccessoryFilter compares each declared field by exact string equality and
  treats an absent attribute as "don't care", so an element with no attributes
  claims every accessory the phone is ever switched into accessory mode for.
  Claiming narrowly looks tidier and produces the single worst outcome this
  project can have: a head unit that identifies itself with strings we did not
  predict never launches us at all, and the person in the car sees nothing
  happen. Nothing happening is indistinguishable from a bad cable, a
  charge-only port, an unlicensed head unit and a bug in our code — four
  different fixes behind one symptom.

  Claiming everything converts that into data. UsbAttachActivity records the
  six identifying strings and refuses to start a session for anything it does
  not recognise, so the widened filter changes what we learn, not what we do.
  The cost is that we appear in the chooser for other accessories; on a phone
  running a de-Googled ROM with no other projection app installed, that is a
  price worth paying for the strings.

  The specific entries below are kept because they document what a head unit is
  expected to send, and because they are what UsbAccessoryTransport.isHeadUnit
  matches on.
-->
<resources>
    <usb-accessory manufacturer="Android" model="Android Auto" />
    <usb-accessory manufacturer="Android" model="Android Open Automotive Protocol" />
    <usb-accessory />
</resources>
